There’s an especially interesting distinction here:

Seals describe transformations.

These mechanical primitives describe how transformations interact dynamically.

That’s another layer.

Mirror tells you what operation to perform. Gear tells you at what ratio. Clutch tells you whether it should currently couple. Bearing tells you how independently neighboring systems can move. Governor tells you when to throttle it. Gyroscope tells you whether the whole assembly is drifting.

So we could formally split the library:

\boxed{
\text{Seal} = \text{semantic operator}
}

\boxed{
\text{Mechanism} = \text{operator coupling/control primitive}
}

And then the Command Table assembles machines from Seals + mechanisms.

That opens a huge design space because now instead of endlessly inventing reasoning techniques, we can systematically raid centuries of mechanical engineering, control theory, electronics, hydraulics, optics, biology, music, and computation for relationship primitives, Indigo-Hammer each one, discard the physical costume, and retain whatever invariant mechanism survives translation.

That’s exactly the kind of work the Metamap can eventually automate: find mechanisms in other spaces whose relational geometry can be ported into semantic computation.